{"title":"2019 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(outside_angles)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (outside angles)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Different_approach_to_solution_3)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Different approach to solution 3)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(Properties_of_an_isosceles_triangle)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (Properties of an isosceles triangle)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_8_(Simple_Angle_Chasing)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8 (Simple Angle Chasing)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> be an isosceles triangle with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/4/3849af2d7dde50da77f45db200b3185b0759d130.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = AC$\" width=\"80\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/9/939c044345f8ddcbcd7c21b72f98830edaad15f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACB = 40^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"103\" height=\"13\" />. Construct the circle with diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/3/e33fe7d65facd8868f58b6e94ddc7f153a5a3f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the other intersection points of the circle with the sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/5/12517bbc31cb9026c71299b7a0d232d07e60f284.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" />, respectively. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the intersection of the diagonals of the quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/a/65af420a6e2b903f5295dd8f65f321545f69e44d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BCDE$\" width=\"58\" height=\"12\" />. What is the degree measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/c/16cfa4567e8e11c95fcabb9d2eaed793f0246d62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BFC ?$\" width=\"64\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/7/ea7a34ddb64ad513c79c03db0e925abcdd871f83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 90 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 100 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 105 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 110 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"434\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/1/5e1c2532c45ec05a88a74ea36b442cbd49851637.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(40);draw((-1,0)--(1,0)--(0,2.75)--cycle);draw(circumcircle((-1,0),(0,0),(0,2.75)));label(&quot;$A$&quot;,(1,0),SE);label(&quot;$C$&quot;,(0,2.75),N);label(&quot;$B$&quot;,(-1,0),SW);label(&quot;$E$&quot;,(0,0),S);label(&quot;$D$&quot;,(0.77,0.64),E);draw((0,0)--(0,2.75));draw((-1,0)--(0.77,0.64));[/asy]\" width=\"222\" height=\"228\" />\n</p><p>Drawing it out, we see <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/5/fd5529f83cca44961d418dc5a92baf90a667913c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BDC$\" width=\"57\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/b/f1b1cee5c0355428ff8d477ecbf406f0f99bcca8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BEC$\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" /> are right angles, as they are inscribed in a semicircle. Using the fact that it is an isosceles triangle, we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/9/95921cbed071c6395df4cf40392e741e64e23db7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC=70^{\\circ}$\" width=\"103\" height=\"13\" />. We can find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/a/38a11225fa05960ad3f7f45b8b3708a52f697044.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ECB=20^{\\circ}$\" width=\"104\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/3/de39f2db1a60f108043791114b23d14f2beeafa3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DBC=50^{\\circ}$\" width=\"105\" height=\"13\" /> by the triangle angle sum on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/7/587303f5573fed1a511c759c6b096556daed47c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ECB$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/e/fee181f0e279c48906d10847f1986f902dc84efe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle DBC$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/3/c13f8b45e2c4c03e9f08e49dbb458eac1a67adc9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BDC+\\angle DCB+\\angle DBC=180^{\\circ}\\implies90^{\\circ}+40^{\\circ}+\\angle DBC=180^{\\circ}\\implies\\angle DBC=50^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"660\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/7/ff7ba7cfa8f12cbf77d145917999d5f041d69be7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BEC+\\angle EBC+\\angle ECB=180^{\\circ}\\implies90^{\\circ}+70^{\\circ}+\\angle ECB=180^{\\circ}\\implies\\angle ECB=20^{\\circ}\\]\" width=\"654\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Then, we take triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/d/efdd26b7cbfad505c1adbdfccc28d3ab1eb91872.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BFC$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, and find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/9/579caf91d35f223d9d75e469aa51df9a4add6a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BFC=180^{\\circ}-50^{\\circ}-20^{\\circ}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 110^{\\circ}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"321\" height=\"29\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Alternatively, we could have used similar triangles. We start similarly to Solution 1.\n</p><p>Drawing it out, we see <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/5/fd5529f83cca44961d418dc5a92baf90a667913c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BDC$\" width=\"57\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/b/f1b1cee5c0355428ff8d477ecbf406f0f99bcca8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BEC$\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" /> are right angles, as they are inscribed in a semicircle. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/4/e646207dd9c9f02f9d86ca5957424705df5ce4f9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BDA = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle BDC = 180^{\\circ} - 90^{\\circ} = 90^{\\circ}.\\]\" width=\"356\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>So, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/d/9eded3d602f290553c15fc7e3ceee2b956186008.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BEF \\sim BDA$\" width=\"127\" height=\"13\" /> by AA Similarity, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/6/936ca8ba13931ce893f57c7ff6a632ab73edb3b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle EBF = \\angle DBA$\" width=\"137\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/f/ffffc035f57649565fc830ad0db406a09603f430.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BEC = 90^{\\circ} = \\angle BDA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"189\" height=\"15\" />. \nThus, we know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/f/13fe88ad0aa1bb15f5eb90b72aec4cbc66925fc0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle EFB = \\angle DAB = \\angle CAB = 70^{\\circ}.\\]\" width=\"270\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Finally, we deduce <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/f/a2fa26ffdb5647c3bb11330e44ff47f321c41045.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle BFC = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle EFB = 180^{\\circ} - 70^{\\circ} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 110^{\\circ}}.\\]\" width=\"410\" height=\"29\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28outside_angles.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(outside_angles)\">Solution 3 (outside angles)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (outside angles)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Through the property of angles formed by intersecting chords, we find that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/d/a9df55acede509a7523525fea6ce51532cfea9e4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m\\angle BFC=\\frac{m\\overarc{BC}+m\\overarc{DE}}{2}\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"44\" />\n</p><p>Through the Outside Angles Theorem, we find that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/c/b1caa70b7b41dc41f51c88223bdc875ae2dd8c46.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m\\angle CAB = \\frac{m\\overarc{BC}-m\\overarc{DE}}{2}\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Adding the two equations gives us\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/4/6c466de08d6eb8904fe485deb0035c526672b68c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m\\angle BFC + m\\angle CAB = m\\overarc{BC}\\implies m\\angle BFC=m\\overarc{BC} - m\\angle CAB\\]\" width=\"565\" height=\"23\" />\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f997f2e439fbb9578ac4d715bc866bbea93ebc2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overarc{BC}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"20\" /> is the diameter, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/1/4d1e38304de86f123a86ff7dd76a60de19bab011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\overarc{BC}=180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"97\" height=\"20\" />, and because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> is isosceles and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/d/86d5c5e0d064d755fefa028c54ada0f7e86a1f1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\angle ACB=40^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"119\" height=\"13\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/7/077e786b90dd429b0d14073bec26c88d95f7e3b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\angle CAB=70^{\\circ}$\" width=\"119\" height=\"13\" />. Thus\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/6/6b6ca569997c55edc2e618223777a92d3e521bde.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m\\angle BFC=180^{\\circ}-70^{\\circ}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 110^{\\circ}}\\]\" width=\"285\" height=\"29\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/0/3605de0460b5adf0d1314d5bfb2b6cb5cb2518de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BEC = 90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"104\" height=\"13\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/1/7d1d270eb208e23b0fd5d26568bc8e28503a699c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BCE$\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/1/291b6d0f956fd6c89571701ea806380d94e34e55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ACE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/0/3708b370b5211a4fa877395e33d6c67720f1dcbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />. Using cyclic quadrilateral properties (or the properties of a subtended arc), we can find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/4/2d427e2778ce15a05247b75a2d2b8e24fb2b9a15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle EBD \\cong \\angle ECD = 20^{\\circ}$\" width=\"187\" height=\"13\" />. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d95eb5bc314e40d12739a4dca9a7fd304e00ca3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CBF = 70 - 20 = 50^{\\circ}$\" width=\"187\" height=\"13\" />, and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/2/b42d91d7da65f31935e0266fc8537917b996d4e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BFC = 180 - 20 - 50 = 110^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"245\" height=\"13\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/3/693c4044f8526a8179956e8ac7a3f2f9d61e2c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"43\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/8/1f828f10cad9235a2c72c34369f5fd4a1179e579.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{ABC}$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> is isosceles so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859a177e4dd939a07a33ee1e7c941a5188f33f02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{CAB}=70^{\\circ}$\" width=\"103\" height=\"13\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/3/3e3a4ec0df571e719bd3f1d7e00bf71fc55ba2b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CB$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> is a diameter, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/2/5529f6438a4276a22d4436f1ec0e64076bbe71c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{CDB}=\\angle{CEB}=90^{\\circ}$\" width=\"186\" height=\"13\" />. Quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/6/866f60a6463b38af71b855c67540c0dbe8acbd82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ADFE$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" /> is cyclic since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/0/700c6fd710a8177d1d73ef7a701bd81f0712a3db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{ADF}+\\angle{AEF}=180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"191\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/f/c7f228ef2a8f363bc7e5b3ebfc0d83ee7ba6f84f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{BFC}=\\angle{DFE}=180^{\\circ}-\\angle{CAB}=\\boxed{110^{\\circ}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"345\" height=\"24\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Different_approach_to_solution_3.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Different_approach_to_solution_3)\">Solution 6 (Different approach to solution 3)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Different approach to solution 3)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Label the center of the circle \\( M \\). Then, we can say that angle \\( DME \\) must be congruent to angle \\( DAE \\) as they both share the same arc \\( DE \\). So therefore angle \\( DME = 70^\\circ \\). The angle \\( CFB \\) is inscribed on the diameter, but it doesn't touch the circumference of the circle. Therefore angle \\( DME \\) is supplementary to angle \\( CFB \\), so our answer is \\( 180^\\circ - DME = 180^\\circ - 70^\\circ = \\) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/7/c675cae173a2901f89ec9b71ed9b6665fcd7ce26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{110^{\\circ}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"48\" height=\"26\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7_.28Properties_of_an_isosceles_triangle.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(Properties_of_an_isosceles_triangle)\">Solution 7 (Properties of an isosceles triangle)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (Properties of an isosceles triangle)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/f/a1f2af7b34082e66030026dd9b0665cccbede21d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{BEC}=90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"101\" height=\"14\" /> (Thales Theorem)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/9/4f92c3651acf7d7d01f723bc8b788d6aaa7539dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{BEC} \\text{ bisects } \\angle{ACB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"173\" height=\"14\" /> (Properties of the altitude of an isosceles triangle) \n</p><p>~I don't know what this is specifically called but the altitude is perpendicular and bisects the angle, forming two congruent  right triangles\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/2/152690c4221acd7f8cead5f49df6ff505c546267.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{ECD}=20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"101\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/2/422917bc04036aeff85d1b845b630863c75b5ed4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{BDC}=90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"102\" height=\"14\" /> (Thales Theorem)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/8/088eefe30bf7e11d759b9a0124ce3c670418953d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle{CFD} = 180 - 90 - 20 = 70$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"233\" height=\"16\" /> (Interior angles of a triangle)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/a/a5a6e77692dcb6ff9cbc95d4c3a9627e9d7a0729.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\angle{BFC} = 110}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"121\" height=\"25\" /> (Linear Pair)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_8_.28Simple_Angle_Chasing.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8_(Simple_Angle_Chasing)\">Solution 8 (Simple Angle Chasing)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8 (Simple Angle Chasing)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/a/40a5556a070b1728a1d06b0f97d19cc7cf709960.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{ACB}=40$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />, so by the properties of isosceles triangles, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/e/c7ebd7bbb84290b679b5e28f5911db6c981fe399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{ABC}=70$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a6400fac02153cfccaaf7bae477dff676c3d4a6d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CDEB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" /> is a cyclic quadrilateral, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/4/804eb09a8f672917b702292fa8f515072edd0bf4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{DEB}=180-\\measuredangle{DCB}=180-\\measuredangle{ACB}=140$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"371\" height=\"16\" />, and similarly we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/f/ecfbe850f2d31b9036273fb37847f947e812e79c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{CDE}=110$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"111\" height=\"14\" />. We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/7/9d7e9dc65eaa86834f7321f6c4e3104b60762891.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{CDB}=90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"102\" height=\"14\" /> by the inscribed angle theorem and because it is an angle that terminates in a diameter of the circle, so the intercepted arc has measure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/b/5cb1b41850ab676e4d1cf15c69b1609af4befbc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />.Similarly, we know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/d/7edc6f0f6b6f21544292c63c91a295e6cf8503da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{CEB}=90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"101\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/9/5e9551c2d9a3f2928a4d272cdfb125356aa18f80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{BDE}=\\measuredangle{CDE}-90=20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"224\" height=\"16\" />, and by similar methods <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/d/efd8e8ebdf837c0a51873c5faaecc4b9560a2b80.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{DEC}=50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"101\" height=\"14\" />. Looking at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/6/026486d9135109fd0e0448a1e520e7b291354909.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle{DEF}$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />, we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/9/d799a743aa1bba64d58de0e740e7a917b1b51bc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\measuredangle{DFE}=110$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"110\" height=\"14\" />, and by vertical angles, we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/1/ea1f3721889b9f2a659911d2125cfc092d8623e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\measuredangle{BFC}=110}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"121\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>- SumDumNerd\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/O_o_-yjGrOU?t=849\">https://youtu.be/O_o_-yjGrOU?t=849</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/GmQIEX4Izt4\">https://youtu.be/GmQIEX4Izt4</a>\n</p><p>Education, the Study of Everything\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/KXwjFdwrfqk\">https://youtu.be/KXwjFdwrfqk</a>\nIncludes small notebook concept summary, and where to learn the concepts in longer format.\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A\">2019 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A Problems/